2014: R9 290X shipped with 8GB of VRAM;
2025: Nvidia and AMD are still shipping cards with 8GB of VRAM.
That's the same thing if in 2014 there were cards released with 256MB of VRAM which was enough in 2004. What a wonderful time we live...

@Garrus-t4b I've got four computers to benchmark AMD and Nvidia GPUs, two with 4gb VRAM 16gb RAM, one with 8gb VRAM 16gb RAM, and my main one with 24gb VRAM and 64 GB RAM. I'm aware slower system memory can be used when VRAM is exhausted. The numbers are consistent on all these computers at the same settings and resolution. VRAM utilization is the same until we start getting to those few games that exceed 8gb.

Unless you make a habit of maxing out every single brand new game with 4K max settings, you're not hitting 8gb VRAM. 99% of Steam games can't possibly hit 8gb VRAM without going 8K resolution or high-res texture packs. I prefer 1440p maxed settings because the fps boost is much better than the fidelity increase of 4K, so my VRAM is usually more like 4-6gb

RTX 5060 can still play those VRAM-heavy games you're thinking of, just with small sacrifices in resolution and medium textures. 8gb VRAM is fine for now, but might struggle in 2-3 years. If you want to future proof go with 12gb or higher, otherwise you might start running into trouble in a few years. None of my friends have more than 8gb VRAM and they're playing all the same new games I am, just at 1080p usually. RTX 5060 is many times better than the almost-decade old cards they're using

